{"qid":"Q28370","names":["Goguryeo"],"title":"Goguryeo","endonym":null,"capsule":"Goguryeo was one of the three ancient Korean kingdoms, traditionally founded in 37 BC in the northern Korean peninsula and Manchuria; it controlled the largest territory of the three kingdoms at its apex. Under King Gwanggaeto the Great (r. 391–413) the kingdom expanded aggressively, conquering Liaodong from Later Yan, subduing Baekje, and receiving Silla as a tributary, giving it dominion over most of Manchuria and the northern half of the peninsula. His son Jangsu (r. 413–491) moved the capital to Pyongyang in 427 and extended control southward to the Han River. After two failed Sui invasions in 612 and 614, a combined Tang–Silla alliance finally conquered Goguryeo in 668, ending over seven centuries of statehood.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial outline is based on textual sources (Chinese annals, Samguk Sagi) and some epigraphic evidence (Gwanggaeto Stele); boundaries during the Manchurian expansion are reasonably well attested, but precise frontier lines in the steppe periphery remain reconstructive.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Goguryeo","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Goguryeo"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Goguryeo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goguryeo"}],"britannica":"place/Koguryo","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Goguryeo","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Dongmyeong","nativeName":"동명성왕","roman":"Dongmyeongseong-wang","start":-37,"end":-19,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Yuri","nativeName":"유리왕","roman":"Yuri-wang","start":-19,"end":18,"im